e from the  hackneyed storytelling in frequent story  missions do at least latch on to the  pomp and spectacle of paybacks fast and  furious inspiration but it falters here  too all these moments of vehicle-based  history on its feature its most visually  arresting set pieces they're frequently  spoiled when the game rips control away  from you just as things are getting  interesting in favor of a canned  cutscene this might look more cinematic  but being forced to watch the scene you  could feasibly control from behind the  wheel is incredibly disheartening and  feeds into paybacks inherent dullness  [Music]  my previous Need for Speed games you'll  pull up the checkpoints within the open  world with race off-road drift drag and  run events providing plenty of variety  race an off-road event speak for  themselves and drift competitions offer  an anomalous thrill getting your car  sideways is so easy that there's a  singular pleasure in simply seeing how  long you can maintain a drift for while  drag events are short and sweet but soon  grow repetitive as you're essentially  just staring at a meter to time your  gear shifts meanwhile simple runner  events focus on police chases and time  dashes between various points on the map  the problem is outside of drift events  where it's a non-factor the sense of  speed in payback is startlingly lacking  the speedometer may say so but there's  no tangible sense that you're hurtling  down the road at 160 miles per hour in a  few guzzling death-trap  motion blur attempts to sell a fast pace  but never quite captures a sensation  that the wards rushing by it's all  surprisingly pedestrian with passive AI  and police chases that are elementary  the cops might be aggressive but you  never have to out think or outmaneuver  them escaping is as simple as reaching a  specific checkpoint this all combines to  make a game that's bland and  unfulfilling and is only exacerbated by  the sheer amount of grinding you're  required to do each vehicle you own has  six slots for speed cards that represent  various pods under the hood and come  with perks that will increase particular  stats and your cars overall level each  event has a recommended level and since  there's no scaling this means you'll  usually have to spend upwards of 40  minutes or so collecting speed cards to  upgrade your car you're essentially  reaching these frustrating choke points  where the only way to progress through  the story is by going back and replaying  old events in order to earn enough speed  cards to bump up your level and when you  factor in the ability to expedite the  process with microtransaction loot boxes  it feels geared towards encouraging you  to part with real money unless you want  to spend time replaying lukewarm parts  of the game over again  can it you know most it's not everyday  got to take where's dB 11 first school  need for speed payback spinel racing is  only magnified by this focus on grinding  the simple almost retro handling model  provides occasional bouts of fun but  it's never enough to escape paybacks  flaws with an unwillingness to let you  partake in its most hair-raising moments  and a general drabness that seeps into  every layer of the game fast and furious  this is not and that's a disappointing  outcome  [Music]  you

